DEEP[1]=('Chapter 16 is named Daivāsura-Sampad-Vibhāga Yoga, the yoga of distinguishing the divine and demonic endowments. <i>Sampad</i> means wealth: the qualities are a person’s true riches or debts.',
 'Śaṅkara says fearlessness comes first because one who knows the Self has nothing to fear; the Taittirīya Upaniṣad (2.9) says the knower of Brahman’s bliss fears nothing. Rāmānuja says it means freedom from fear of losing what one loves, by trusting the Lord. Gandhi called <i>abhaya</i> the first requirement of a satyāgrahī.',
 'Courage, purity and honesty: begin with these, and the other qualities follow.')
DEEP[2]=('',
 '<i>Ahiṃsā</i> and <i>satya</i>, non-violence and truth, were the twin pillars of Gandhi’s life. Patañjali lists them as the first two <i>yamas</i> (Yoga Sūtra 2.30, 2.35–36). <i>Apaiśuna</i>, not slandering, and <i>acāpala</i>, not fidgeting or being fickle, are small everyday virtues. Commentators note how practical the list is: one can begin any of these today. <i>Hrī</i>, modesty, is the healthy shame that stops wrongdoing.',
 'Don’t speak ill of anyone today. That one practice changes relationships.')
DEEP[3]=('',
 'Twenty-six qualities in three verses. Commentators note the balance of <i>tejas</i>, energy and radiance, with <i>kṣamā</i>, forgiveness: the divine nature is not weak. <i>Adroha</i>, not wishing harm to anyone. <i>Nātimānitā</i>, “not too much pride”: some self-respect is fine. Many Hindu schools teach these qualities to children; Vinoba Bhave wrote on them at length. The list matches the marks of knowledge in 13.7–11.',
 'Strength and forgiveness belong together. Practise both.')
DEEP[4]=('',
 'Six demonic qualities against twenty-six divine. Commentators note hypocrisy comes first, as fearlessness did on the divine side. <i>Asura</i> in the Vedas originally meant a powerful lord; later it came to mean the opponents of the gods. In the Gītā it is a character, not a species. The Chāndogya Upaniṣad (8.7–12) tells how Indra and the asura Virocana both studied with Prajāpati. Virocana left thinking the body was the self; Indra stayed and learned the truth.',
 'Watch for hypocrisy and harshness in yourself first, before you see them in others.')
DEEP[5]=('',
 '<i>Mā śucaḥ</i>, “do not grieve”, appears here and in 18.66, the Gītā’s final word of comfort. Commentators find this moment tender. Arjuna, hearing the demonic list, may fear he has some of these traits, after his anger at the Kauravas. Kṛṣṇa reassures him. Śaṅkara says Arjuna’s readiness to fight in a just war is not demonic, because it is duty. Rāmānuja says the Lord’s love speaks here.',
 'If you worry about being good, you are probably already on the right path. Keep going.')
DEEP[6]=('',
 'Commentators say the divine nature has been described throughout the Gītā: in the sthita-prajña (2.55–72), the devotee (12.13–20) and the guṇātīta (14.22–25). The demonic nature is now described in 16.7–20 so that it can be recognised. The Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad (1.3.1) says gods and asuras are both children of Prajāpati, competing in the world. Many teachers say both are within each person.',
 'Knowing the shape of a trap helps you avoid it. Study your own lower tendencies with honesty.')
DEEP[7]=('',
 '<i>Pravṛtti</i> and <i>nivṛtti</i> are also the two great paths of Hindu life: active engagement and withdrawal. Here they mean knowing when to act and when to restrain oneself. Commentators say the demonic nature lacks this discernment. Śaṅkara says they do not know right and wrong as taught by scripture. Rāmānuja says they do not distinguish the means to good from the means to harm.',
 'Knowing when to say no is as important as knowing when to say yes.')
DEEP[8]=('',
 'The Lokāyata or Cārvāka school taught that only the senses give knowledge, the body is the self, and pleasure is the goal. A famous line attributed to them: “while you live, live happily; borrow and drink ghee; once the body is ash, how can it return?”. Commentators of all schools criticised this view. The Gītā’s concern is ethical: a view of the world with only desire at its centre tends toward cruelty and greed.',
 'What we believe about the world shapes how we treat it. Notice your own deepest assumptions.')
DEEP[9]=('',
 '<i>Alpa-buddhi</i>, small understanding, sees only the self and its pleasures. Commentators say such a view, put into action, harms others. Śaṅkara says they become enemies of the world because they act only for themselves. The epic gives examples: Duryodhana’s schemes, Śakuni’s tricks, Kaṃsa’s killing of infants. The verse warns that ideas have consequences.',
 'A narrow view of life can do great harm. Widen your view to include others’ good.')
DEEP[10]=('',
 '<i>Duṣpūra</i>, hard to fill, recalls 3.39, where desire is a fire that cannot be filled. Commentators note <i>aśuci-vrata</i>, impure vows: the demonic also make resolutions, but for selfish ends. Śaṅkara says they adopt wrong ideas because of delusion. The story of King Yayāti, who borrowed his son’s youth to continue enjoying and still found no end to desire, is a classic illustration.',
 'Notice when “enough” never comes. That is the warning sign.')
DEEP[11]=('',
 'Commentators note the pairing: endless pleasure-seeking and endless worry go together. Śaṅkara says those who think pleasure is all live in constant anxiety about gaining and keeping it. Rāmānuja contrasts this with 9.22, where the devotee’s yoga-kṣema is carried by the Lord. <i>Etāvat</i>, “this much is all”, is the creed of a closed mind.',
 'Chasing pleasure brings worry with it. Contentment brings peace. Notice which you are feeding.')
DEEP[12]=('',
 '<i>Āśā-pāśa</i>, ropes of hope or expectation. A verse of the Bhaja Govindam says: the body withers, the hair turns grey, the teeth fall out, yet the grip of hope does not let go. Commentators note that <i>artha</i>, wealth, is a legitimate aim of life in Hindu thought; only <i>anyāya</i>, injustice, is condemned. The verse is about greed’s methods, not wealth itself.',
 'Earn honestly. Wealth gained unjustly binds the one who gathers it.')
DEEP[13]=('',
 'Verses 13–15 are a monologue of the ego. Commentators note the repeated <i>me</i>, “mine”, and the endless future tense: always more to come. The first word of the Gītā’s first speaker, <i>māmakāḥ</i> (1.1), returns here in a fuller form. The ideal of 2.71 and 12.13, <i>nirmama</i>, “without mine”, is the opposite. Śaṅkara says this is how the demonic think day and night.',
 'Listen to the “mine, more, mine” in your own head. Smile at it, and let it soften.')
DEEP[14]=('',
 '<i>Īśvaro’ham</i>, “I am the lord”, is the ego claiming the place of God. Commentators contrast it with 11.33, where Arjuna is told he is only an instrument: “they are already slain by me”. The demonic say “slain by me”; the devotee knows who truly acts. Śaṅkara says such people claim lordship that belongs to the Supreme. Rāmānuja says they forget their dependence on the Lord.',
 'When “I” swells, something has gone wrong. Return to gratitude.')
DEEP[15]=('',
 'A subtle point: religious acts, sacrifice and charity, are part of the demonic monologue. Commentators explain that the acts are done for status. The Gītā will classify such giving as rājasika or tāmasika in 17.20–22. Śaṅkara says even sacrifice performed to outshine others is demonic. Many traditions warn against piety for show; the Sermon on the Mount says, “when you give alms, do not sound a trumpet”.',
 'Good deeds done for show lose their goodness. Give quietly.')
DEEP[16]=('',
 '<i>Moha-jāla</i>, the net of delusion: a fish struggling in a net only tightens it. Commentators read <i>naraka</i>, hell, both literally, as the Purāṇic hells of punishment, and as the inner hell of a tormented mind. Śaṅkara says the demonic, after death, fall into hells such as Vaitaraṇī. Many modern teachers stress the inner reading: addiction and endless craving are already a kind of hell.',
 'Many scattered thoughts make a net. Simplify, and the knots loosen.')
DEEP[17]=('',
 '<i>Nāma-yajña</i>, sacrifice in name only. Commentators note that the outer show of ritual is there, but the spirit is gone. Śaṅkara says they perform sacrifices just to be called sacrificers. The Gītā consistently values motive over appearance (17.11–13). Many saints, like Kabīr, criticised religious display without sincerity.',
 'Rituals mean something only with sincerity. Bring your heart to whatever you practise.')
DEEP[18]=('',
 'This verse makes a profound point: hating others is hating the Lord in them, and even in oneself. Commentators link it to 13.28: one who sees the Lord everywhere does not harm the Self by the self. Śaṅkara says the demonic violate the Lord within by ignoring scripture. Rāmānuja says they hate the Lord as inner controller of all bodies. <i>Abhyasūyaka</i>, one who finds fault with goodness, appears also in 3.32 and 9.1.',
 'When you feel hatred for someone, remember who lives in them. It may soften the feeling.')
DEEP[19]=('',
 'Commentators handle this hard verse carefully. Śaṅkara says the Lord gives each being the fruit of its own karma; the first-person voice means the Lord administers the law. Rāmānuja says the Lord, as the just ruler, places beings according to their deeds, without hatred (9.29). The phrase <i>ajasram</i>, “constantly”, shows how habits perpetuate themselves. Modern readers often take it as a psychological truth: cruelty breeds more cruelty.',
 'Cruelty breeds more cruelty, for the cruel above all. Each act of kindness breaks the cycle.')
DEEP[20]=('',
 'Commentators of the bhakti schools stress <i>mām aprāpya</i>, “not reaching me”: the deepest tragedy is missing the Lord. Yet the Gītā elsewhere promises that even the worst can turn (9.30–31), and the Purāṇas tell of demons who became devotees: Prahlāda, Bali, Vibhīṣaṇa. Śaṅkara says the downward path continues only while the demonic nature remains. No state is permanent.',
 'No state is final. This verse, read with 9.30, leaves room for return.')
DEEP[21]=('',
 'This verse is among the most quoted in the Gītā. Commentators note how the three are linked: desire blocked becomes anger (2.62), desire satisfied becomes greed for more. Śaṅkara says these three are the root of all demonic qualities. Buddhism names greed, hatred and delusion as the three roots of unwholesome action. Gandhi called this verse a practical summary of ethics.',
 'Watch for desire, anger and greed in yourself today. Closing even one gate makes a difference.')
DEEP[22]=('',
 'Commentators note the positive turn. Freedom from the three gates is not the end but the beginning: then one naturally does what is truly good, <i>śreyas</i>. Śaṅkara says such a person follows the path of scripture and reaches liberation. Rāmānuja says such a person comes to know and love the Lord. The word <i>śreyas</i> recalls Arjuna’s question in 2.7.',
 'Every time you let go of anger or greed, you take a step toward the light.')
DEEP[23]=('',
 '<i>Śāstra</i> means scripture, but also any body of trusted teaching. Commentators contrast guided action with impulsive action. Śaṅkara says scripture shows what we cannot learn by perception alone, such as the fruits of actions after death. Rāmānuja says scripture is the Lord’s command for our good. Many modern teachers widen the meaning to include the accumulated wisdom of the wise. Arjuna’s next question (17.1) follows from this verse.',
 'Impulse is a poor guide. Lean on wisdom that has been tested over time.')
DEEP[24]=('',
 '<i>Pramāṇa</i>, a valid means of knowledge. Indian philosophy counts perception, inference and testimony among the <i>pramāṇas</i>; scripture is testimony about what the senses cannot reach. Commentators note that the Gītā does not ask for blind obedience; it reasons with Arjuna throughout, and in 18.63 tells him to reflect and choose. The chapter ends by setting up chapter 17. The colophon names chapter 16 Daivāsura-Sampad-Vibhāga Yoga.',
 'Have a standard you trust more than your moods. It steadies decisions when feelings run high.')
